{"parentUuid":"75f443d5-0855-4900-87a9-b851d28942ec","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01TUVEZekCUrYaLh3VNvYT4o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user makes a good point. On Gabia hosting, if a file doesn't exist, it would throw a 404. The fact that it's throwing a 503 \"Service Unavailable\" with the Apache message means... \n\nWait, let me reconsider. The KILL_SWITCH is set to `true` in config.local.php. When KILL_SWITCH is true, bootstrap.php does:\n```php\nhttp_response_code(503);\nheader('Retry-After: 3600');\nheader('Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8');\n\n\n```\n\nBut the user is seeing an HTML \"Service Unavailable\" page, not JSON. \n\nHmm... could it be that Gabia intercepts the 503 status code and replaces the response with their own HTML 503 page? That's actually possible! Some hosting providers override 503 responses with their own error pages. If Gabia is intercepting the 503 HTTP status and replacing the body with their own HTML error page, then this IS our PHP running correctly with KILL_SWITCH=true — the hosting provider is just masking what we're actually sending back.\n\nSo the KILL_SWITCH is working as intended, setting the 503 status code correctly. Gabia's just overlaying their own error page on top of our response. Now I can move forward with step 4 and set up the noltheater config.\n\n```","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":807,"cache_read_input_tokens":42353,"output_tokens":685,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":807,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":42353,"cache_creation_input_tokens":807,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_i